  "notes": "West leads the ♦K against 4♠. South has a possible trump loser if East holds the ♠K. West's ♦K lead has set up a trick in that suit for the defense. There is also a risk of losing 2 club tricks if West holds the ♣A.\n\nWest has shown the ♦Q with his ♦K lead. South can establish the ♦J as a discard for one of his possible club losers. If South wins the ♦A and immediately returns the ♦2, West will win the ♦Q and North's ♦J will be high.\n\nIf West returns a trump after winning the ♦Q, South should decline the finesse, rising with North's ♠A. South can afford to lose a trick to the ♠K. However, he cannot afford to lose a trick to East before he discards a club on North's ♦J. If East has the ♠K and West the ♣A, finessing in spades would be fatal.\n\nSouth should play the ♦J from dummy and take his club discard before continuing spades. South will lose just 2 more tricks, the ♠K and the ♣A.",
